=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported inter-VLAN routing fault between VLAN 20 (172.16.20.0/24, laptop0)
and VLAN 10 (172.16.10.0/24, pc4) does not exist in the live lab. All evidence
confirms the network is fully healthy.

Directly measuring the data plane: ping from laptop0 (172.16.20.10) to pc4
(172.16.10.4) returns 4/4 packets with 0% loss at TTL=63, and the reverse
direction (pc4 → laptop0) is equally successful at 4/4 with 0% loss. TTL=63
confirms that packets are crossing exactly one routing hop — router0 — as
expected for inter-VLAN routing.

On router0, both sub-interfaces are operational: eth0.10 carries 172.16.10.1/24
and eth0.20 carries 172.16.20.1/24, both in UP state. The kernel forwarding
table shows direct connected routes for both subnets. There are no iptables
rules (all chains are policy ACCEPT with zero entries), and the nftables ruleset
contains only Docker-internal DNS NAT entries scoped to 127.0.0.11 that have no
effect on routed traffic. The FRR running configuration on router0 contains only
RIP network statements with no access-lists, prefix-lists, or distribute-lists
that could filter inter-VLAN traffic in either direction. Both endpoints have
correct default gateways pointing to router0.

The premise of the helpdesk ticket — that router0 dropped the eth0.20
sub-interface or that an ACL is blocking VLAN 20 → VLAN 10 — is false. No
corrective action is required.
=== END ===
